Bashnative currently provides:

  [ arch base64 basename cat chmod clear cp curl cut date dirname du
  echo editor env expand expr false find fold grep groups head hexdump
  hostname id kill ln logname ls man md5sum mkdir mkfifo mknod mktemp
  more mv nc nl nohup nproc od paste printenv printf pwd readlink
  realpath reset rev rm rmdir seq sleep sort split stat strings sync
  tail tee test timeout touch tr true tty uname unexpand uniq uptime
  users wc wget whereis which whoami xargs yes [1]


Build system:

  build/build-bash.sh compiles a patched bash binary with the 'mk'
  builtin.  The 'mk' builtin provides mkdir(2), mkfifo(3), and
  mknod(2) syscalls that cannot be done in pure bash.

  Usage: cd build && ./build-bash.sh [--static] [-j4]

  The resulting bash binary + all scripts in bin/ is a complete
  minimal userland.  One binary.  Everything else is a text file.



Underlying philosophy:

  Main reason; just for fun.

  We do not recommend this collection ( or the memory model and runtime
  speed of the Bourne Again SHell ) for production systems.

	To provide a minimal system to be used in, for instance, chrooted
	environments it can be, and already has proven so, an alternative to
	bigger systems.

	Open source userland:
	No compilation necessary, not ever, a text editor is all you need. [2]


Three-pronged approach for missing syscalls:

  The 'mk' function (function/mk) automatically picks the best method:
  1. mk builtin   - patched bashnative bash (zero overhead)
  2. enable -f     - stock bash + compiled loadable (no fork per call)
  3. external bins - /usr/bin/mkdir etc (works anywhere, but forks)



[1] disclaimer: even though the programs are similar in functionality as
    their named counterparts, they are not exact copies.  Furthermore some
    are just links, for instance, vim, vi, nano and pico all link to the
    editor program. These links are just there for usability reasons.

[2] well, almost.  The 'mk' builtin for mkdir/mkfifo/mknod requires one
    initial compilation of bash.  After that, everything is text files.
